WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.050
Okay weíre going to be looking today at copper.
This is something looking back at the old

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:09.889
video, itís a short video and there isnít
any experimentation in it so I thought weíd

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:12.889
do something a bit practical.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:18.090
Copper is an element which people are very
familiar with because itís such a characteristic

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:24.810
coloured metal, sort of reddish colour, though
it gets darker when oxide forms. But itís

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:30.200
quite a soft metal and you can cut it quite
easily if you have a cutter like this which

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:38.609
has steel blades, it will just dig into the
metal and it should just cut off. When you

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:46.319
cut the metal you get this very nice, shiny,
fresh surface coloured just like copper.

00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:51.930
This is a ball of copper, copper metal. As
you might imagine itís a ball of metal so

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.520
therefore it is quite dense and heavy. I mean
itís not as dense as, I think Steve told

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.961
me this morning, that if we had a ball this
size of plutonium he told me it would weigh

00:00:59.961 --> 00:01:04.030
about a kilo and a half so he thought. So
itís not quite as heavy as that but still

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.049
itís pretty heavy and dense.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:11.380
So this is a very, very nice sample of very
fine copper wire. So the copper wire here

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.450
is, what width is this? This isÖ phew! Point
zero, Iím not even going toÖthatís just

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:21.119
too small, so itís point one of a millimetre.

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:27.110
So inside the beaker weíve got a solution
called sodium potassium tartrate, so no copper

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.869
in there at the moment. Weíre going to introduce
that through this. This is a solution of copper

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:32.920
sulphate.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:39.570
Copper is very popular for architectural use
because if you put it on the roofs of buildings

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:46.119
it then reacts, particularly with carbon dioxide
in the air, to give a very nice green colour.

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:52.299
First of all though we need to heat this to
about 50∞C, and to the sodium potassium

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:58.039
tartrate we need to add some very weak, hydrogen
peroxide so this is only 3% hydrogen peroxide,

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:04.579
so in it goes. We need to make sure they mix
together which is why thereís a stirring

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:15.150
bar at the bottom there stirring away. Okay
so thatís stirring nicely, so as I said sodium

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:22.209
potassium tartrate and hydrogen peroxide and
we need to add only one mill so thatís 1cm3

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:24.470
of the copper sulphate.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.331
The copper wire here is very, very high grade,
this is grade one copper wire and itís point

00:02:29.331 --> 00:02:33.780
one of a millimetre in diameter and you can
see the wonderful copperas lustre coming off

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:34.799
the side there.

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:36.299
What do you mean by grade one copper?

00:02:36.299 --> 00:02:43.120
So this is very high purity, this is like
99.99% copper, used for electronics fabrications

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.090
and high quality research samples.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:52.629
Itís also used for electrical wiring, in
fact itís used so much that in a country

00:02:52.629 --> 00:03:01.870
like the UK each person has about 175 kilos
of copper associated with each person, in

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:08.440
the piping, the wiring and so on. The problem
is that in many other countries, for example

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.930
in China, people have very much less copper
associated with them, there isnít so much

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:21.629
electrical wiring in rural parts of China.
And if everybody in China had the same amount

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:28.220
of copper associated with them as those in
England or America or Canada there wouldnít

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.590
be enough copper in all the known deposits
around the world.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:38.250
Copper is actually necessary for life, plants
and animals use copper. But actually you can

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:43.959
have a disease called Wilsonís disease which
basically your body canít metabolise copper

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:45.349
properly, itís actually an inherited disease.

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:57.099
Ready? Whatís going on there is itís producing
a lot of gas so itís reacting to form a complex.

00:03:57.099 --> 00:04:04.519
And itís going from the potassium tartrate
complex there to well, its producing carbon

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:09.090
dioxide and oxygen, mainly oxygen gas is being
produced which is why youíre seeing the fizzing.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:16.130
And itís become copper oxide, cuprous oxide
actually.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:23.720
Copper is found as a metal lying around in
various places in the world. I believe the

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:30.000
name came from the island of Cyprus in the
middle of the Mediterranean, but copper has

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.660
always been know even in pre-historic times
people found small amounts of copper. But

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:43.090
as we use more and more copper people have
had to dig deeper and deeper mines. The mine

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:50.150
in Chile where those very brave miners were
entombed for more than two months and then

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:57.430
rescued was a copper mine. And to get the
metal that we need weíre going to have to

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.250
go deeper and deeper and deeper into the earth.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.660
So what weíve got in there now is cuprous
oxide itís copper 1 oxide. And itís actually

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:11.280
gone from what was a kind of clear see-through,
all right itís got a colour on it, but see-through

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.750
solution to something thatís very cloudy.
So what weíve got there is a precipitate

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.900
of copper oxide.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:24.880
Copper is very good because it has very high
thermal and electrical conductivity. What

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:33.169
that means is that heat flows through it very
well and also electricity. If you make wires

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:39.630
for electrical transmission out of copper,
when the electricity goes through them you

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:50.140
get very much less heating than say if you
made the wires out of iron, and all this heating

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:58.330
is of course lost energy. Youíd do even better
if you made the wires out of silver, but silver

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.460
is too expensive.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:09.900
OK. So letís see if we can change this reaction
back. So at the moment it started out as being

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:15.830
about 50∞C and itís shot up to about 63∞C,
I imagine it was even hotter than that when

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:21.729
it originally changed colour. So weíll see
if we can take it back, Iíll put that back

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:22.729
in.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:26.750
I actually bought this from the British Geological
Survey which is just down the road from here,

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:31.300
I think it is in Keyworth I think. And I just
saw this and it was a Christmas present from

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.259
my husband and I picked it out. I just think
itís really cool actually.

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:37.830
What did your husband think of getting that
as a Christmas present?

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:42.800
He knows what Iím like! So he buys these
things for me he knows Iím a bit of a magpie,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.280
I like to collect shiny things, and pretty
things, thatís my copper.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:53.750
So the colour is gradually changing, itís
gone from orange and now kind of a murky yellow.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:07:01.810
You can see itís kind of green now. And again
a gradual change, it wasnít a sudden spontaneous

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:06.990
straight back to being blue and see-through,
you know clear. Now weíve got back to that

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.889
clear solution. And again you can see on the
stirrer bar all the bubbles of the oxygen,

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:14.069
little bit of C02 forming on that.

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:20.729
Most organisms, you and me, use iron in our
blood to transport oxygen, thatís what makes

00:07:20.729 --> 00:07:30.300
our blood red. But in some sorts of crustaceans,
crabs and lobsters, theyíre different they

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:37.419
use copper. They use a compound called hemocyanin,
itís a blue compound of copper. Cyanine is

00:07:37.419 --> 00:07:45.970
some classical word for blue. So they really
have blue blood. And these hemocyanin use

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:53.710
two atoms of copper in each molecule to transport
the oxygen around the body of the crab or

00:07:53.710 --> 00:08:00.290
lobster. But itís not nearly as good as haemoglobin,
so if you start running after a crab or lobster

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:05.570
it quickly gets tired compared to running
after a rabbit or something like that, which

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:07.769
can keep going faster than you can.

